<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martti Rautanen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1845–1926</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nakambale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martti Rautanen was a Finnish Lutheran missionary who worked among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovambo-people">Ovambo</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> from 1870 until his death in 1926. Based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olukonda">Olukonda</a> in Ondonga, where he was known as Nakambale, he translated the Bible and other texts into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndonga-dialect">Oshindonga</a>, founded the first congregations and helped make Ondonga largely Christian. He also collected plants and wrote on Ovambo customs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martti-rautanen.jpg" alt="Martti Rautanen" width="320" height="531" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rautanen1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
